# SAP revision to-do workflow

When working through items in `sap-revision-todo.md`:

## Traceability comments

Whenever you add or change SAP text for a to-do item, place an HTML comment **immediately above** the edited passage in the source `.qmd` (or shared include), citing the to-do ID and originating comment IDs:

```html
<!-- sap-revision-todo A1 (comments id=199 KH, id=200 AO): short rationale. -->
```

- Use the item ID from `sap-revision-todo.md` (e.g. `A1`, `B3`).
- Include the comment IDs and reviewer initials given in the to-do item when available.
- Keep the rationale to one short clause.
- Do this for every substantive edit made for a to-do item, including shared includes under `../shared-assets/`.

## Style and scope of edits

- Make **minimal edits** to the SAP: change only what the to-do item requires; do not rewrite surrounding prose, restructure sections, or add optional elaboration.
- Match the **style and tone** of the existing SAP text: concise declarative sentences, plain scientific English, same terminology and level of detail as neighbouring paragraphs.
- Prefer inserting or adjusting a short clause or sentence over adding long justificatory paragraphs.
- When replacing a passage, read adjacent sections first and mirror their voice.

## Completion gate

- Do **not** mark a to-do item complete (in `sap-revision-todo.md` or the session todo list) until the user has explicitly approved the edits.
- Items tagged **[decision]** need a human judgement call before editing — present options; do not guess.
- Items tagged **[verify]** may already be satisfied — confirm current text before proposing changes.

## Protocol alignment

Before proposing or finalising a substantive SAP edit, check the corresponding text in `../protocol/protocol.qmd` (and shared includes used by both documents).

- **Default: keep the SAP aligned with the protocol** so the protocol need not change. Prefer wording that elaborates or operationalises the protocol without contradicting it.
- **Do not edit shared includes under `../shared-assets/` that are also used by the protocol** (e.g. `outcomes.qmd`) unless the user explicitly agrees: those edits revise the protocol. Prefer SAP-only text (e.g. analysis sections, `tables/outcomes-summary.json`) when clarifying for the SAP.
- Flag any **material deviation** from the protocol (analysis model, estimand, estimation method, sensitivity analyses, analysis sets, outcome definitions).
- When a reviewer request conflicts with the protocol, first propose an SAP formulation that satisfies the review **and** stays within the protocol. Only if that is impossible, present the conflict explicitly and ask whether to (1) keep the protocol-aligned SAP wording, or (2) accept a minimal protocol edit later.
- **Do not edit the protocol during the SAP revision.** If an approved SAP change unavoidably conflicts with the protocol, add a **minimal** item to `protocol-edits-todo.md` (cite the SAP to-do ID and protocol location). That list is a last resort; apply it only after the SAP revision is finished.
- Keep any deferred protocol edits as small as possible (one sentence or phrase). Skip the list when the SAP merely elaborates compatible protocol text.
- Detail that elaborates the protocol without changing its substance is fine; changing method class (e.g. time-to-event vs count, RSPL vs Laplace) is material.

## Source of truth

- Edit `statistical-analysis-plan.qmd` or the shared include, not rendered copies.
- After edits, re-render when the user asks or when needed to confirm the document still builds.
